=========================================================
SOLID PRINCIPLES – INTERVIEW NOTES (FINAL)
==========================================

SOLID is a set of five Object-Oriented Design principles that help build software that is:

• Easy to maintain
• Easy to extend
• Loosely coupled
• Highly cohesive
• Testable
• Scalable

=========================================================
S → SINGLE RESPONSIBILITY PRINCIPLE (SRP)
=========================================

## Definition

"A class should have only ONE reason to change."

## Meaning

One Class = One Responsibility = One Job

## Interview Example

Bad

class Invoice
{
public:
void calculateTotal();
void printInvoice();
void saveToDatabase();
};

Good

class Invoice
{
public:
void calculateTotal();
};

class InvoicePrinter
{
public:
void printInvoice();
};

class InvoiceRepository
{
public:
void saveToDatabase();
};

## Why?

Calculation, printing, and database operations are different responsibilities.

## Interview Answer

"SRP states that a class should have only one responsibility and therefore only one reason to change."

## Remember

One Class = One Job

=========================================================
O → OPEN CLOSED PRINCIPLE (OCP)
===============================

## Definition

"Software entities should be OPEN for extension but CLOSED for modification."

## Meaning

Add new functionality without modifying existing code.

## Interview Example

Bad

class Payment
{
public:
void pay(string type)
{
if(type=="CreditCard")
{
}
else if(type=="UPI")
{
}
else if(type=="PayPal")
{
}
}
};

Good

class Payment
{
public:
virtual void pay() = 0;
};

class CreditCard : public Payment {};
class UPI : public Payment {};
class PayPal : public Payment {};

Need ApplePay?

class ApplePay : public Payment {};

## Why?

Adding a new payment method requires creating a new class instead of modifying existing code.

## Interview Answer

"OCP means software should be open for extension but closed for modification."

## Remember

Extend, Don't Modify

=========================================================
L → LISKOV SUBSTITUTION PRINCIPLE (LSP)
=======================================

## Definition

"Derived classes should be replaceable with their base class."

## Meaning

A child class should work correctly wherever the parent class is expected.

## Interview Example

Bad

Bird
|
Penguin

Bird has fly().

Penguin cannot fly.

Good

Bird

FlyingBird
|
Sparrow

Penguin : Bird

## Alternative Classic Example

Rectangle → Square

## Why?

Only classes that truly satisfy the parent's behavior should inherit from it.

## Interview Answer

"LSP states that derived classes should be usable wherever the base class is expected without changing program correctness."

## Remember

Child should correctly replace Parent.

=========================================================
I → INTERFACE SEGREGATION PRINCIPLE (ISP)
=========================================

## Definition

"Clients should not be forced to depend on methods they do not use."

## Meaning

Create small, focused interfaces.

## Interview Example

Bad

class Machine
{
public:
virtual void print() = 0;
virtual void scan() = 0;
virtual void fax() = 0;
};

SimplePrinter must implement scan() and fax() unnecessarily.

Good

class Printer
{
public:
virtual void print() = 0;
};

class Scanner
{
public:
virtual void scan() = 0;
};

class Fax
{
public:
virtual void fax() = 0;
};

SimplePrinter implements only Printer.

## Why?

A class should implement only the operations it actually needs.

## Interview Answer

"ISP recommends creating small, focused interfaces so clients implement only the functionality they require."

## Remember

Small Interfaces

=========================================================
D → DEPENDENCY INVERSION PRINCIPLE (DIP)
========================================

## Definition

"High-level modules should not depend on low-level modules.
Both should depend on abstractions."

## Meaning

Depend on interfaces, not concrete implementations.

## Interview Example

Bad

class Computer
{
Keyboard keyboard;
};

Good

class InputDevice
{
public:
virtual void input() = 0;
};

class Keyboard : public InputDevice {};
class Mouse : public InputDevice {};
class TouchScreen : public InputDevice {};

class Computer
{
private:
InputDevice* device;
};

## Why?

The Computer can work with any input device without changing its code.

## Interview Answer

"DIP states that high-level modules should depend on abstractions rather than concrete implementations."

## Remember

Depend on Abstractions

